# The Pi's Compatible build (docs/IMAGES.md §2): what it still does not
# build, on top of common/ and the board's lists. DRM is for the display
# only, sound is built only because vc4 depends on it, and HID and USB
# storage are what the PC has, no more. KCMP, which common/ no longer
# forbids, is not in this list: DRM selects it, and Faraday carries no
# kernel patch of its own to take the select out (F153).

# The display only.
DRM_V3D                 the Pi 4's 3D driver; nothing here renders on the GPU (the Pi 3's V3D is part of vc4, and its render node is root's, F153)
DRM_VC4_HDMI_CEC        HDMI-CEC, the monitor's remote-control bus into the device
CEC_CORE                the layer under it, named so it cannot come back under another driver
DRM_DP_AUX_CHARDEV      raw DisplayPort AUX access from a process
DRM_LEGACY              the pre-KMS DRM interfaces, which let a process program a GPU directly
DRM_LOAD_EDID_FIRMWARE  an EDID from a file in place of the screen's own

# Sound: the core vc4 depends on, and no driver.
SND_USB                 a USB sound card is a device this signs with nothing on
SND_BCM2835             the firmware's analogue and HDMI audio over VCHIQ
SND_BCM2835_SOC_I2S     the SoC's I2S block, for a sound card on the header
SND_DRIVERS             the generic sound drivers: loopback, dummy, MIDI
SND_SEQUENCER           the MIDI sequencer
SND_OSSEMUL             the old OSS interface to the sound core

# Input and storage as on the PC (boards/x86_64-uefi/kernel.forbidden).
HIDRAW                  raw HID reports; input reaches the shell through evdev
USB_HIDDEV              as HIDRAW
HID_APPLE               each vendor HID driver binds to IDs any USB device can claim and parses its reports with its own code; HID_GENERIC covers the input
HID_A4TECH              as HID_APPLE
HID_BELKIN              as HID_APPLE
HID_CHERRY              as HID_APPLE
HID_CHICONY             as HID_APPLE
HID_CYPRESS             as HID_APPLE
HID_EZKEY               as HID_APPLE
HID_GYRATION            as HID_APPLE
HID_ITE                 as HID_APPLE
HID_KENSINGTON          as HID_APPLE
HID_LOGITECH            as HID_APPLE
HID_REDRAGON            as HID_APPLE
HID_MICROSOFT           as HID_APPLE
HID_MONTEREY            as HID_APPLE
HID_NTRIG               as HID_APPLE
HID_PANTHERLORD         as HID_APPLE
HID_PETALYNX            as HID_APPLE
HID_SAMSUNG             as HID_APPLE
HID_SONY                as HID_APPLE
HID_SUNPLUS             as HID_APPLE
HID_TOPSEED             as HID_APPLE
HID_PID                 as HID_APPLE
SCSI_LOWLEVEL           the SAS, iSCSI, fibre-channel and RAID controllers a disk can be behind; a USB stick is behind none of them
BLK_DEV_SR              binds to any USB device presenting a CD-ROM and parses what it sends
CHR_DEV_SG              raw SCSI commands from a process to any disk
USB_UAS                 a second USB storage protocol with its own parser; usb-storage reads every stick
USB_SERIAL              a serial adapter is a way in, not a device this signs with
USB_ACM                 as USB_SERIAL
